Found 2026-09-21 while migrating a downstream project, the corpus this guard was built
against.

The guard fires correctly. Its remedy line does not work:

  Run 'bd export' there and try again, or --anyway to import what is here.

Two things go wrong following it literally.

`bd export` with no arguments writes JSONL to **stdout**. It does not touch
.beads/issues.jsonl. So the first try changes nothing and the guard repeats
itself unchanged, which reads like the guard is broken.

`bd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl` does write the file — and does **not**
update .beads/export-state.json. That sidecar is bd's bookkeeping for its own
automatic export, and no bd subcommand refreshes it on demand (checked the
full command list: no sync; export's only flags are --all, --include-infra,
--no-memories, -o, --scrub).

So the remedy moves the failure rather than clearing it:

  before   contains  445 issues
           104 ticket(s) the interaction log names are not in it
  after    contains  445 issues
           declares  332, so the file and its own state disagree

The second message is accurate and is the more useful of the two. But there
is now no way to satisfy this guard through bd, so every real migration ends
at --anyway, which is the escape hatch rather than the path. A guard whose
only exit is its own override teaches people to reach for the override.

The file itself was genuinely fresh in that second state — exported minutes
earlier. Only the sidecar was stale. That is the case worth separating: the
count check reads export-state.json, and it is the one input that bd will not
refresh.

Worth considering: when the id-based check passes (no ticket the interaction
log names is missing from the file), the file is demonstrably current and a
disagreeing sidecar is a fact about bd's bookkeeping rather than about the
export. That is the sharper signal, and the README already says so.

Whatever the fix, the remedy line should say -o and name the file.


Done 2026-09-24. With an interaction log, it alone decides: if it names
nothing the file lacks, a count that disagrees with export-state.json is
printed as a note and the import goes ahead. Without a log the count still
refuses, and the message says bd export -o does not refresh the count, so
--anyway is the way through for a file just exported. Both remedy lines now
say bd export -o and give the full path to issues.jsonl. The reference text
says the same, and tests/migrate.sh covers all three cases.

Not re-run against the downstream project: its .beads is gone, so the real corpus was not
available.
